From c4d56e8375edc0849bbe4a8354ec8fb97ac11365 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(players): expose native emotes to client integrations

Decode Bedrock 1.26.51 EmotePacketPayload and EmoteListPacketPayload using
the target protocol 2193 field order. Preserve unsigned tick lengths and
resolve actor runtime IDs through the player entity tracker. Discard the
Xbox and platform identity fields after decoding; client animation only
needs the player UUID, emote UUID, length, and flags.

Register an EmoteProvider for direct client playback. Its default sends
versioned payloads only to clients that register viabedrock:emote, so a
proxy can support compatible clients without inventing Java animations.
Keep unmodified clients' behavior unchanged. Tests verify field order and
unsigned length boundaries against the target schema.

Source: https://github.com/Mojang/bedrock-protocol-docs/blob/v1.26.51/json/EmotePacketPayload.json
Source: https://github.com/Mojang/bedrock-protocol-docs/blob/v1.26.51/json/EmoteListPacketPayload.json

Retain protocol 2193 StartGame emote-chat muting in the game session so
client integrations can honor the native world announcement policy.

Forward native world emote-chat muting in the existing payload flags so a
ViaProxy client does not need access to the proxy's GameSessionStorage.
Compare playback lengths unsigned before forwarding them, including values
whose unsigned Bedrock representation has the Java sign bit set.

Accept negotiated local emote controls through the Java connection. Keep
advertised IDs and bounded playback state per user, choose actor and Xbox
identity from that session, and clear Emoting on stop, expiry or capability
withdrawal. Native packets retain zero outgoing flags. Send the world chat
policy before advertising the action channel so local proxy announcements
honor it without a local Bedrock tracker.

Transport native announcement names alongside the world chat policy. Java
profiles carry synthetic names, and the local Java UUID can differ from
the native player-list UUID. Resolve the local entry by native unique ID
and retain its connected-account name as the pre-list fallback. Keep the
existing playback payload compatible while supplying this context only
to clients that register its new channel.
